name: cacio e pepe
source: https://www.youtube.com/shorts/XuNv9nk4VbE
uses:
  - carbs/fresh-pasta
  - carbs/pasta
yield: serves 2
notes: |
  Pasta, pecorino, black pepper, pasta water. No cream, butter or oil — it's all technique, and
  the technique is all about not clumping the cheese.

  Temperature is the dish. Pecorino's proteins seize and squeeze out their fat above ~80°C, so
  water straight off the boil at 100°C curdles it into stringy lumps in a grey puddle. Everything
  that touches the cheese goes in at ~70°C / 150°F.

  Agitation is the other half. You're forcing an emulsion of cheese fat, water and starch, and it
  only forms under mechanical work — stir hard with two forks, and the more you mix the creamier
  it gets. Undermixed cacio e pepe is thin.

  Quantities are standard proportions; the 60-second source doesn't state its own.
ingredients:
  - item: pasta
    mass: 200
    basis: true
    note: DRIED weight. for fresh use 250-300g — see the fresh pasta component before scaling
  - item: pecorino romano
    mass: 120
    note: real Pecorino Romano DOP. microplane it — box-grater shreds clump, full stop
  - item: black pepper
    qty: 1.5
    unit: tsp
    note: whole peppercorns, cracked coarse yourself. see the pepper component
  - item: salt
    note: for the water, lighter than usual — pecorino is very salty
components:
  - name: pepper
    notes: |
      Coarse isn't needed for the bloom to work — fine extracts faster — it's needed for the
      result. Fine grind over-extracts to acrid and turns the sauce grey and dusty. Coarse gives
      aromatic bursts and speckle without harshness, and blooming is what compensates for its
      slower extraction. Mortar and pestle or a pan-bottom crush beats a grinder's coarsest
      setting. Pre-ground is dead — the volatile aromatics are half the dish.

      The water bloom pulls piperine and aromatics into the liquid, so the pepper dissolves
      through the sauce instead of sitting on it as grit. No fat in this dish, so water extracts.
    steps:
      - dry-toast the WHOLE peppercorns over medium (160°C) for 60-90 secs until sharp and floral. toasting whole is the point — ground pepper scorches and loses its aromatics before it reaches the pan
      - cool 2 mins, then crack coarsely. a grinder on its coarsest setting is fine; grinding them hot gums the burrs
      - return to the pan with 3-4 tbsp pasta water and swirl 30-60 secs to a fragrant slurry
      - this water stays in the pan and becomes the base of the sauce — it is never tipped out. the only water discarded is the pot you drain the pasta from
      - "no mortar or grinder: peppercorns on a board, heavy flat-bottomed pan on top, lean your weight on it and ROCK. rocking cracks, hammering powders them and fires them across the kitchen"

  - name: method
    notes: |
      Pan timeline: hot for the toast and bloom, hot for the pasta toss, then OFF and never back
      on. A thin stainless pan helps — it sheds heat fast. Cast iron holds enough residual heat to
      curdle the cheese after it leaves the burner; give it a full minute off, or tip into a
      warmed bowl.
    steps:
      - boil the pasta in lightly salted water, using less water than usual — a smaller volume goes far starchier, and starch is what stabilises the emulsion
      - ladle out a mug of pasta water and let it cool to ~70°C, a minute or two. use a thermometer the first few times to calibrate what that looks like
      - toast and bloom the pepper (above) while the pasta cooks
      - grate the pecorino into a bowl and add the 70°C water a splash at a time, stirring to a smooth paste the consistency of thick cream
      - if it's grainy now it will be grainy on the plate — work it smooth before the pasta goes near it
      - drain the pasta just shy of al dente, add to the bloomed-pepper pan with a ladle of water, toss over medium 30-60 secs to finish it and reduce the water to syrupy
      - take the pan off the heat and wait ~30 secs to drop to 65-70°C
      - add the cheese paste and stir vigorously with two forks, tossing and lifting, until glossy and coating every strand. keep going past the point it looks done
      - loosen with pasta water if it tightens. never return it to the flame to thicken — that's how it splits
      - serve immediately in warmed bowls with more pepper. it tightens as it cools and there's no rescuing it
  - name: temperature-controlled pan (breville control freak)
    notes: |
      Removes the one thing that makes this dish hard. The failure is heat overshoot at the cheese
      stage, and here you just hold 68°C and stir as long as you like without it splitting.

      The Control Freak reads the pan base, so contents sit a touch below the setpoint — close
      enough that the numbers below are the contents temperature you want. Use medium intensity
      throughout; fast overshoots on the way to target, which is the one thing to avoid.

      It heats but can't cool. Coming down from the 90°C toss to the 68°C emulsion, lift the pan
      off the hob for ~30 secs rather than waiting for it to coast — a thin stainless pan sheds
      that 20°C quickly off the element, and slowly on it.

      Thick clad base (aluminium core) changes both ends of that. Better at the hold: it's far
      more stable once equilibrated, so 68°C is rock solid, and 160°C toasts the pepper dead even
      with no scorching. Worse through the drop: the base is a heat reservoir and the Freak's
      sensor reads the base, not the food, so it keeps dumping stored heat into the contents after
      the setpoint falls — the contents sail past 68°C while the display says you're there. So
      set the hob to 65°C rather than 68 and let residual carry it up, lift for 60-90 secs rather
      than 30, and toss the pasta at 85°C rather than 90 so there's less to shed. Simplest of all:
      take it off the hob entirely for the cheese stage — a thick clad pan holds ~68°C unaided for
      several minutes, far longer than the stir takes. Back on the 65°C hob only if it drags.
    steps:
      - "160°C, medium intensity — dry-toast the whole peppercorns, 60-90 secs. nudge to 175°C if nothing is happening by 2 mins"
      - "100°C — add the 3-4 tbsp pasta water to the cracked pepper and bloom to a slurry, 30-60 secs"
      - "90°C — add drained pasta and a ladle of water, toss 30-60 secs to finish it and reduce to syrupy"
      - lift the pan off the hob for 30 secs and set the hob to 68°C
      - "68°C, held — return the pan, add the cheese paste and stir hard with two forks until glossy. this is the whole advantage: it cannot creep past 80°C while you work, so stir as long as you like"
      - "the separate mug of water for the cheese paste still needs to hit ~70°C off-appliance. let it stand and check with a thermometer — don't dilute it with cold water to get there, you'd be thinning out the starch that stabilises the emulsion"
      - "pasta pot, if it's on the Freak: 100°C, fast intensity"
  - name: with fresh pasta
    notes: |
      Fine here, unlike aglio e olio — it's the authentic version. Roman tonnarelli IS fresh egg
      pasta: a square-section strand cut from a sheet rolled thicker than tagliatelle.

      Flour: 70:30 or 50:50 00 to semola rimacinata, same egg ratio. Durum brings the firm toothy
      bite, which is the fix for the softness problem rather than a compromise on it. Not all
      semolina with egg — that's chitarra dough, stiff to roll, and you lose the egg's tenderness.
      All 00 or all AP still makes good tonnarelli; just don't roll it thin.

      Square means cross-section — spaghetti with corners, not a folded or filled shape. Flat
      faces and sharp edges hold a clinging emulsion far better than a round strand's single
      tangent of contact. Roll the egg dough from carbs/fresh-pasta to 2.5mm (vs 1-1.5mm for
      tagliatelle) and cut at the same width. A machine spaghetti cutter won't take a sheet this
      thick, so cut by hand — easy at this width. The traditional tool is the chitarra, a
      wire-strung box you press the sheet through with a pin.

      Thickness is doing real work: this is a sharp, salty, one-note sauce and it needs chew to
      play off. With the flour blend you can go to 2mm for a more sauce-forward result, but not
      below — the square section stops reading and the cook drops to ~60 secs, leaving no margin.
      Hold at 2.5mm the first time; thicker is the forgiving direction.

      Weights don't convert 1:1. The 200g above is dried, which is ~12% moisture and drinks water
      as it cooks; fresh already carries its egg. 100g dried is 125-150g fresh per person, so two
      people want 250-300g of fresh tonnarelli — about 190g of flour's worth, a bit over a third
      of the 500g batch in carbs/fresh-pasta.

      Don't enter the fresh weight as the basis — the excess over the dried figure is water, not
      pasta, and everything scales up with it. Convert first: fresh x 0.75 = dried equivalent.
      That holds up against the dry matter, e.g. 200g flour (~14% moisture, ~172g dry) plus 110g
      egg (~25% solids, ~27g dry) is ~199g dry matter, which at dried pasta's ~12% moisture is
      ~226g dried equivalent — near enough the 232g the 0.75 rule gives.

      So a 310g fresh batch goes in as ~232g, which scales the pecorino to ~139g.

      Two adjustments: fresh pasta water goes starchy much faster so it emulsifies readily, but
      add it more cautiously or the sauce goes slack. And fresh pasta keeps drinking sauce after
      plating — finish it looser than looks right and serve immediately.
